{"cuisine/circassian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/circassian-cuisine","n":"Circassian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Caucasian cuisine","cuisine/caucasian-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Russia","intro":[["Circassian cuisine is an ethnic cuisine, based on the cooking style and traditions of the Circassian people of the North Caucasus. This region lies between the Black Sea and the Caspian Sea, within European Russia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"circassian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circassian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Caucasian cuisine","cuisine/caucasian-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Caucasian cuisine","cuisine/caucasian-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b7/Old_Circassian_traditional_house.jpg/500px-Old_Circassian_traditional_house.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"ShapsougSochi1864","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Circassian_traditional_house.jpg"}},"cuisine/turkish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/turkish-cuisine","n":"Turkish cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Turkey","intro":[["Turkish cuisine encompasses the traditional and regional foods of Turkey and its diaspora. It developed primarily from Ottoman cuisine, which synthesized Central Asian culinary traditions with Mediterranean and Middle Eastern influences. During the Ottoman period, Turkish cuisine played a central role in influencing the cuisines of the former Ottoman territories, particularly in the Balkans, the Levant, Mesopotamia, North Africa, the South Caucasus and Crimea, resulting in a shared culinary heritage."],["The cooking of Istanbul, Bursa, İzmir, and the rest of the Anatolia region inherits many elements of Ottoman court cuisine, including moderate use of spices, a preference for rice over bulgur, koftes, and a wider availability of vegetable stews (türlü), eggplant and stuffed dolmas. The cuisine of the Black Sea region uses fish extensively, especially the Black Sea anchovy (hamsi) and includes maize dishes. The cuisine of the southeast (including Urfa, Gaziantep, Adıyaman and Adana) is famous for its variety of kebabs, mezes and dough-based desserts such as baklava, şöbiyet, kadayıf, katmer and künefe."],["Especially in the western parts of Turkey, where olive trees grow abundantly, olive oil is the primary type of cooking oil. The cuisines of the Aegean, Marmara and Mediterranean regions are rich in vegetables, herbs, and fish. Central Anatolia has many well-known specialties, such as keşkek, mantı (especially from Kayseri) and gözleme. Food names directly cognate with mantı are also found in Chinese (mantou or steamed bun), and it is generally considered to have originated in Mongolia during the 13th century."],["Specialties are often named for places, and may refer to different styles of preparation. For example, Urfa kebap is less spicy and thicker than Adana kebap. Although meat-based foods such as kebabs are common in Turkish cuisine abroad, meals in Turkey largely center around rice, vegetables, and bread."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"turkish-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":30,"links":[["Adana kebabı","dish/adana-kebab"],["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Bici bici","dish/bici-bici"],["Boortsog","dish/boortsog"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Çiğ köfte","dish/cig-kofte"],["Çökertme kebabı","dish/cokertme-kebab"],["Doner kebab","dish/doner-kebab"],["Düğün çorbası","dish/dugun-corbas"],["Eggplant jam","dish/eggplant-jam"],["İmam b