"color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"channelislands.coop","note":"Local co-operative's collection of traditional Channel Islands dishes.","url":"https://www.channelislands.coop/food/recipes/liberation-day-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/07/Pais_au_fou%2C_Jersey_bean_crock.jpg/500px-Pais_au_fou%2C_Jersey_bean_crock.jpg","w":500,"h":393,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Man vyi assumed (based on copyright claims).","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pais_au_fou%2C_Jersey_bean_crock.jpg"}},"ingredient/herbs":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/herbs","n":"Herbs","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Herbs are a widely distributed and widespread group of plants, excluding vegetables, with savory or aromatic properties that are used for flavoring and garnishing food, for medicinal purposes, or for fragrances. Culinary use typically distinguishes herbs from spices. Herbs generally refers to the leafy green or flowering parts of a plant (either fresh or dried), while spices are usually dried and produced from other parts of the plant, including seeds, bark, roots and fruits."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-herbs"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbs"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Ajika","dish/ajika"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Khmeli suneli","ingredient/khmeli-suneli"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/27/Garden_of_herbs.jpg/500px-Garden_of_herbs.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Lauren 240","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garden_of_herbs.jpg"}},"ingredient/squid-as-food":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/squid-as-food","n":"Squid as food","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 6 cuisines","intro":[["Squid is eaten in many cuisines; in English, the culinary name calamari is often used for squid dishes. There are many ways to prepare and cook squid. Fried squid is common in the Mediterranean. In Britain, it can be found in Mediterranean \"calamari\" or Asian \"salt and pepper fried squid\" forms in various establishments, often served as a bar snack, street food, or starter. In other Anglophone or Commonwealth countries, such as New Zealand, Australia, the United States, Canada, and South Africa, it is sold in fish and chip shops, Italian and other Mediterranean restaurants; seafood restaurants, and steakhouses."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-squid-as-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Squid_as_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":6,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"]]],["Andorran cuisine","cuisine/andorran-cuisine",[["Arròs negre","dish/arros-negre"]]],["Catalan cuisine","cuisine/catalan-cuisine",[["Arròs negre","dish/arros-negre"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"]]],["Korean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/korean-chinese-cuisine",[["Ulmyeon","dish/ulmyeon"]]],["Valencian cuisine","cuisine/valencian-cuisine",[["Arròs negre","dish/arros-negre"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cuttlefish","ingredient/cuttlefish"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Cephalopod ink","ingredient/cephalopod-ink"],["Cubanelle","ingredient/cubanelle"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],